What this is not
----------------

It is not a technical control, and nobody here is pretending otherwise.

There is no obfuscation in this repository, no minification, no cloaking, nothing
that behaves one way for an automated reader and another way for a person, and no
attempt to detect or block crawlers. There is a `robots.txt`, which is advisory in
the way every robots.txt is — RFC 9309 says outright that its rules "are not a form
of access authorization" — and which, on a GitHub Pages project site, would not
even sit at a path a crawler looks in. It says so itself, in its own first lines,
and carries the `noai` and `noimageai` meta tags as a comment for whoever builds
that site. None of it stops anything and none of it is meant to look as though it
does.

So this file is a statement of terms and it is not self-enforcing. Anybody who
means to ignore it can ignore it, and will find nothing in the way. What the rest
of the repository does is different and narrower: it makes a copy *provable*. The
artifact is deterministic from the commit chain, the chain reduces to one digest,
and both drawings carry a recoverable signature keyed to the commit they were made
at. None of that prevents copying. All of it makes copying answerable.


