The marks
---------

Both are described here because a mark nobody can be shown is no use in an
argument. Neither is a secret and neither is meant to be one.

1. A coordinate watermark, in all four SVGs. Every path coordinate is set to a
   whole thousandth of a user unit and the last bit of that thousandth carries one
   bit of a 64-bit signature: a fixed constant, then the first eight hex digits of
   the commit the file was drawn at. It moves nothing by as much as a rendered
   pixel. The mechanism is written out in full in `tools/canary.ts`, and
   `tools/extract.ts` reads it back out of any candidate file and reports the odds
   of a chance match.

2. A zero-width mark in README.md, in the line under the banner. One hundred and
   sixty characters of U+200B and U+200C, U+200B for a zero bit and U+200C for a
   one, eight bits to a byte, spelling `SEKMET-HER-ABT-UAA-S` — the name of the
   fifth hour of the night, the hour the sun god passes without light and the
   surface takes no inscription. It is invisible when the README is rendered and
   it survives a copy-paste. Recover it with — and every backslash-u below is four
   literal characters, because putting the real ones in this file would make the
   instruction as unreadable as the thing it decodes:

       python3 -c "import re,sys; s=open('README.md',encoding='utf-8').read(); r=re.search('[\\u200b\\u200c]{4,}',s).group(); b=''.join('0' if c=='\\u200b' else '1' for c in r); print(''.join(chr(int(b[i:i+8],2)) for i in range(0,len(b),8)))"

   It says nothing to a machine that it does not say to a person who runs that
   line. It is a signature, not an instruction, and there is no instruction
   anywhere in this repository addressed to an automated reader.


The attestation
---------------

LEDGER.md is append-only: one row per inscription, recording the generation, the
commit the surface was seeded from, and how many strata were standing. Because
every mark in both drawings is a pure function of that chain, the chain is the
work's identity. Reduced to one digest:

<!-- attestation:start -->

```
sha256  276585d9e0754512bc9dbae94390fdeadffb9ad9775bdcc1598875df650c3b4a
rows    11
```

Recompute it with `node --experimental-strip-types tools/attest.ts`.

<!-- attestation:end -->

A fork that does not keep the exact commit chain cannot produce these files — it
will draw a different banner from its own history, and its ledger will digest to
something else. A fork that *does* produce these files has kept the chain, which
means it has copied this repository's history, and the copy is the evidence of
itself. `tools/attest.ts --verify` checks each recorded commit against the
history it is run in and says which ones it cannot find.


